1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,549
A starfish.

2
00:00:01,549 --> 00:00:02,709
A cockroach.

3
00:00:02,709 --> 00:00:03,689
A sponge.

4
00:00:04,089 --> 00:00:06,118
Draw one straight line through each.

5
00:00:06,118 --> 00:00:08,501
Can you split it into two matching halves?

6
00:00:08,951 --> 00:00:12,101
Aarav tried it with a
ruler at his study table.

7
00:00:12,101 --> 00:00:15,024
The starfish split evenly
along several lines.

8
00:00:15,024 --> 00:00:17,141
The cockroach, along only one.

9
00:00:17,141 --> 00:00:18,853
The sponge, along none.

10
00:00:19,203 --> 00:00:21,430
That simple test is called symmetry.

11
00:00:21,430 --> 00:00:26,050
And it hides a deeper story, about how
many layers an animal is built from.

12
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:30,663
By the end, you will know the three
kinds of symmetry, and the difference

13
00:00:30,663 --> 00:00:32,668
between two and three germ layers.

14
00:00:33,318 --> 00:00:35,232
Symmetry means matching halves.

15
00:00:35,582 --> 00:00:38,978
Imagine a flat cut through
the body, called a plane.

16
00:00:38,978 --> 00:00:43,310
If the two sides match like mirror
images, the body is symmetrical along

17
00:00:43,310 --> 00:00:44,210
that plane.

18
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:49,094
Animals fall into three groups:
asymmetrical, radial and bilateral.

19
00:00:49,744 --> 00:00:51,799
Let's test them, one at a time.

20
00:00:52,149 --> 00:00:53,139
The sponge.

21
00:00:53,139 --> 00:00:55,789
Every cut gives two different pieces.

22
00:00:55,789 --> 00:00:57,059
No plane works.

23
00:00:57,059 --> 00:00:59,310
Sponges are mostly asymmetrical.

24
00:00:59,810 --> 00:01:01,124
Now a jellyfish.

25
00:01:01,124 --> 00:01:06,071
Any plane that passes through the central
axis divides it into two identical

26
00:01:06,071 --> 00:01:06,971
halves.

27
00:01:06,776 --> 00:01:08,581
This is radial symmetry.

28
00:01:09,081 --> 00:01:11,262
It is like cutting a round cake.

29
00:01:11,262 --> 00:01:14,413
Every cut through the centre
gives matching halves.

30
00:01:14,413 --> 00:01:17,545
Coelenterates, ctenophores
and echinoderms show it.

31
00:01:18,045 --> 00:01:19,069
The cockroach.

32
00:01:19,069 --> 00:01:23,404
Only one plane, down the middle from
head to tail, gives matching left

33
00:01:23,404 --> 00:01:24,629
and right halves.

34
00:01:24,629 --> 00:01:26,390
This is bilateral symmetry.

35
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:29,304
Annelids and arthropods are bilateral.

36
00:01:29,304 --> 00:01:33,896
Their body has a left and a right
side, a front end and a back end.

37
00:01:34,546 --> 00:01:36,820
Now the second idea, germ layers.

38
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:41,385
For this, we go back to the embryo,
the animal at its earliest stage.

39
00:01:41,735 --> 00:01:42,635
Step one.

40
00:01:42,605 --> 00:01:45,342
The embryo begins as a ball of cells.

41
00:01:46,092 --> 00:01:46,992
Step two.

42
00:01:46,961 --> 00:01:49,080
Its cells settle into layers.

43
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:51,421
The outer layer is the ectoderm.

44
00:01:51,421 --> 00:01:52,883
Ecto means outside.

45
00:01:53,633 --> 00:01:54,636
Step three.

46
00:01:54,636 --> 00:01:57,047
The inner layer is the endoderm.

47
00:01:57,047 --> 00:01:58,498
Endo means inside.

48
00:01:58,498 --> 00:01:59,959
It lines the gut.

49
00:02:00,709 --> 00:02:01,687
Step four.

50
00:02:01,687 --> 00:02:07,058
Some animals stop at two layers, with
a jelly called mesoglea in between.

51
00:02:07,058 --> 00:02:12,238
Mesoglea is undifferentiated: it never
becomes a proper layer of tissue.

52
00:02:12,238 --> 00:02:14,530
These animals are diploblastic.

53
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:15,743
Di means two.

54
00:02:16,493 --> 00:02:17,414
Step five.

55
00:02:17,414 --> 00:02:21,815
Other animals grow a third layer
between the two, the mesoderm.

56
00:02:21,815 --> 00:02:23,222
Meso means middle.

57
00:02:23,222 --> 00:02:25,433
These animals are triploblastic.

58
00:02:25,433 --> 00:02:26,735
Tri means three.

59
00:02:27,485 --> 00:02:31,034
So, coelenterates and
ctenophores are diploblastic.

60
00:02:31,034 --> 00:02:34,192
Platyhelminthes to
chordates are triploblastic.

61
00:02:34,842 --> 00:02:35,894
Remember this.

62
00:02:35,894 --> 00:02:39,946
Ecto is outside, endo is inside,
and meso is in the middle.

63
00:02:40,546 --> 00:02:42,928
Why does a third layer matter so much?

64
00:02:43,278 --> 00:02:48,126
With only two layers, the body is a thin
wall around a gut, like a jellyfish.

65
00:02:48,576 --> 00:02:52,074
The mesoderm fills the space
between the skin and the gut.

66
00:02:52,074 --> 00:02:54,433
It can build muscles and organs there.

67
00:02:54,883 --> 00:02:59,636
That is why triploblastic animals,
from flatworms to us, can build organs

68
00:02:59,636 --> 00:03:01,004
and organ systems.

69
00:03:01,654 --> 00:03:04,427
Let's put the two body plans side by side.

70
00:03:04,777 --> 00:03:09,809
Diploblastic, like Hydra: ectoderm
outside, endoderm inside, and mesoglea

71
00:03:09,809 --> 00:03:10,915
between them.

72
00:03:11,365 --> 00:03:16,259
Triploblastic, like a flatworm: ectoderm,
endoderm, and a real middle layer,

73
00:03:16,259 --> 00:03:17,256
the mesoderm.

74
00:03:17,856 --> 00:03:18,756
Your turn.

75
00:03:18,753 --> 00:03:23,004
A comb jelly, a ctenophore, is
radial and lives in the sea.

76
00:03:23,004 --> 00:03:25,016
Two germ layers, or three?

77
00:03:25,016 --> 00:03:26,336
Pause and decide.

78
00:03:29,536 --> 00:03:30,436
Two!

79
00:03:30,095 --> 00:03:34,111
Ctenophores are diploblastic,
just like the coelenterates.

80
00:03:34,761 --> 00:03:37,514
Now put symmetry and germ layers together.

81
00:03:37,514 --> 00:03:38,836
A pattern appears.

82
00:03:39,186 --> 00:03:43,261
Coelenterates and ctenophores
are radial and diploblastic.

83
00:03:43,711 --> 00:03:49,387
Flatworms, roundworms, annelids and
arthropods are bilateral and triploblastic.

84
00:03:49,837 --> 00:03:53,499
And the odd one out, the
echinoderms, like the starfish.

85
00:03:53,499 --> 00:03:58,484
The adult is radial, but the larva is
bilateral, and the body is triploblastic.

86
00:03:59,134 --> 00:04:00,883
Now, a worked example.

87
00:04:00,883 --> 00:04:02,260
Two body sections.

88
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:03,481
Let's read them.

89
00:04:03,831 --> 00:04:04,845
Section one.

90
00:04:04,845 --> 00:04:07,793
Two cell layers, with jelly between them.

91
00:04:07,793 --> 00:04:09,508
That jelly is mesoglea.

92
00:04:09,508 --> 00:04:10,888
So, diploblastic.

93
00:04:11,338 --> 00:04:16,296
A diploblastic body with radial symmetry
fits a coelenterate, like Hydra.

94
00:04:16,746 --> 00:04:17,820
Section two.

95
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:19,293
Three cell layers.

96
00:04:19,293 --> 00:04:21,398
The middle one is mesoderm.

97
00:04:21,398 --> 00:04:22,915
So, triploblastic.

98
00:04:23,365 --> 00:04:28,109
Add a flat, bilateral body with no
body cavity, and it fits a flatworm,

99
00:04:28,109 --> 00:04:29,180
like Planaria.

100
00:04:29,830 --> 00:04:31,295
Now, the exam trap.

101
00:04:31,645 --> 00:04:34,963
Many students count mesoglea
as a third germ layer.

102
00:04:35,363 --> 00:04:39,677
But mesoglea is an undifferentiated
jelly, not a germ layer.

103
00:04:39,677 --> 00:04:42,527
Coelenterates have only two germ layers.

104
00:04:42,527 --> 00:04:44,198
They are diploblastic.

105
00:04:44,848 --> 00:04:46,351
Where is this asked?

106
00:04:46,351 --> 00:04:50,658
As statement or assertion and reason
questions on the symmetry or germ

107
00:04:50,658 --> 00:04:52,060
layers of a group.

108
00:04:52,660 --> 00:04:53,931
Exam level now.

109
00:04:53,931 --> 00:04:56,419
Read the assertion and the reason.

110
00:04:56,419 --> 00:04:57,744
Pause and solve.

111
00:05:03,144 --> 00:05:04,409
The answer is C.

112
00:05:04,909 --> 00:05:06,544
The assertion is true.

113
00:05:06,544 --> 00:05:09,382
The adult starfish is
radially symmetrical.

114
00:05:09,882 --> 00:05:11,487
The reason is false.

115
00:05:11,487 --> 00:05:14,557
Echinoderm larvae are
bilaterally symmetrical.

116
00:05:14,557 --> 00:05:16,765
Only the adult becomes radial.

117
00:05:17,365 --> 00:05:18,826
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:18,826 --> 00:05:20,327
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:23,527 --> 00:05:26,364
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:05:26,764 --> 00:05:27,664
One.

121
00:05:27,276 --> 00:05:29,538
Which groups show radial symmetry?

122
00:05:30,438 --> 00:05:31,338
Two.

123
00:05:30,938 --> 00:05:34,376
What is mesoglea, and in
which animals is it found?

124
00:05:35,276 --> 00:05:36,176
Three.

125
00:05:35,939 --> 00:05:38,049
What does triploblastic mean?

126
00:05:39,149 --> 00:05:43,508
Next time: an earthworm after rain,
with rings all along its body.

127
00:05:43,508 --> 00:05:45,712
What is hiding under those rings?

128
00:05:45,712 --> 00:05:48,267
We find out in coelom and segmentation.
